menu "espOS NMEA 2000"

    config ESPOS_N2K_CANDUMP_PORT
        int "candump TCP server port"
        default 2599
        range 1 65535
        help
            Default listening port of CandumpTcpServer
            (CandumpTcpServerConfig::port overrides it per instance). 2599 is
            what the SensESP-era gateway listened on and what existing
            canboatjs / SignalK n2k-ip-gateway sources have configured by
            hand; clients that browse mDNS take the port from the record and
            do not care.

    config ESPOS_N2K_RX_QUEUE_DEPTH
        int "TWAI receive queue depth (frames)"
        default 64
        range 8 1024
        help
            Frames the TWAI node's ISR hands to its task before the oldest
            is dropped. A saturated 250 kbit/s bus delivers roughly 1900
            frames a second, so 64 is about 30 ms of backlog -- enough for
            the task to be held off briefly by WiFi or a display refresh,
            not for a stalled consumer. Each slot is one CanMessage (about
            32 bytes).

    config ESPOS_N2K_MDNS_LEGACY_TYPE
        bool "Advertise the legacy _sensesp-n2k._tcp service type"
        default y
        help
            Register the candump server with mDNS as _sensesp-n2k._tcp with
            model=sensesp-n2k-gateway, the names the SensESP-based gateway
            used. They are on the wire: SignalK's n2k-ip-gateway-canboatjs
            source browses for exactly this type and every deployed client
            has it configured, so renaming would make every gateway
            invisible to every client for the sake of tidiness. Off switches
            to _espos-n2k._tcp / model=espos-n2k-gateway, which no released
            client looks for yet.

    config ESPOS_N2K_MAX_LISTENERS
        int "Frame listeners per receiver"
        default 4
        range 1 16
        help
            How many consumers TwaiReceiver::add_listener() can feed from one
            bus: the candump server takes one, an NMEA 2000 node one, and the
            application whatever it adds. Each slot is a std::function
            (16 bytes on these targets) whether used or not.

    config ESPOS_N2K_NODE
        bool "NMEA 2000 node (espos_n2k::Node)"
        default n
        help
            Build espos_n2k::Node, which makes the device a node on the bus
            in its own right: a NAME, the ISO address claim, answers to ISO
            requests, product information, the PGN lists, a heartbeat and the
            device/system instances an MFD can set. It is the NMEA2000
            library (Timo Lappalainen, MIT), vendored under third_party/,
            over this component's receiver and transmitter.

            Off by default because it is not free and a bridge does not need
            it: on adds the library (about 28 KB of flash on esp32s3 with what a
            node uses) and the "NMEA 2000" settings page with the instance keys.
            A pure candump gateway leaves it off and lets the server supply
            the identity.

    if ESPOS_N2K_NODE

    config ESPOS_N2K_NODE_TASK_STACK
        int "Node task stack (bytes)"
        default 4096
        range 3072 16384
        help
            The node task runs the library's ParseMessages() and every
            message listener the application adds to the node.

    config ESPOS_N2K_NODE_TX_QUEUE
        int "Messages Node::send() can queue"
        default 8
        range 2 64
        help
            Node::send() is callable from any task: it copies the message
            into this queue and the node task hands it to the library. Each
            slot is one tN2kMsg (about 250 bytes), so the default costs
            about 2 KB of RAM.

    endif

endmenu
